---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.js';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How RigFloorHQ handles safety-critical material, technical review, standards, and corrections."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Editorial Policy', href: '/editorial-policy' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/editorial-policy"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/editorial-policy" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Editorial</div>
    <h1>Editorial <span>Policy</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">How we write about safety-critical work, who checks it, and what we do when we get something wrong.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Reference, never instruction</h2>
      <p>
        We explain how something works and why. We never tell you what to do on a live well.
        Your employer's procedures, the well programme, the regulations and your certified
        supervisor decide that — always, and without exception.
      </p>

      <h2>Calculators are teaching tools</h2>
      <p>
        The kill sheet, hydrostatic and mud weight window calculators exist so you can see the
        arithmetic and check your understanding while studying. They are not approved for
        operational use, and we say so on the page rather than burying it in the terms.
      </p>

      <h2>Standards are named, not paraphrased into fact</h2>
      <p>
        Where API, IADC, IWCF, OSHA or similar requirements apply, we name them and point you at
        them. Standards get revised; our summary of one is a starting point, not the authority.
      </p>

      <h2>We say when something varies</h2>
      <p>
        Practice differs between operators, contractors, regions and rig types. Where there is no
        single right answer, we say so instead of inventing one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="reviewed-by-a-working-driller">Technical review</h2>
      <p>
        Several of our blog posts and calculators have been checked for technical accuracy by
        <strong>Jabin Travis</strong>, a drilling professional with 30 years of experience across
        conventional and horizontal wells and workover/completions work. See his
        <a href="/about/jabin-travis">full profile, credentials, and everything he's reviewed</a>.
        Pages carrying his review say so visibly and in the page's structured data; pages that
        don't show a reviewer have not yet been checked by him, which is itself worth knowing on
        a safety-critical page.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is not owned by a drilling contractor, an operator, an equipment manufacturer,
        a training provider or a recruitment agency. We carry no advertising and take no payment
        for coverage. A small number of product links are affiliate links, marked as sponsored,
        and they never decide what we recommend or how a page reads.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If something here is wrong, out of date, or explained in a way that would mislead someone
        on a rig, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> and we will fix it. On a site about
        safety-critical work, accuracy is the only thing that makes it worth publishing at all.
      </p>

      <p style="font-size:13px;">
        See also our <a href="/about">About</a> page and <a href="/contact">Contact</a> page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
